// `type_name::<T>()`'s two rejections, both inherited for free from keeping `T` a real type
// reference rather than a string:
//
// * an **unresolvable** type is E0013, resolved exactly like any other annotation — a `type_name`
//   that silently answered `"Ghost"` would be worse than useless, since the whole point is that the
//   string is compile-checked;
// * an erased type **parameter** is E0058, the same rejection `field_specs_of::<T>()` /
//   `construct::<T>(…)` report and for the same reason: one compiled body serves every
//   instantiation, so `T` names no type at run time and this could only ever yield `"T"`.
//
// There is deliberately no forwarding escape here (the way `attributes_of::<T>()` has one):
// `type_name` lowers to a compile-time constant, so there is no runtime node for a hidden
// type-argument slot to feed.
// expect: error E0058 at 17:40
// expect: error E0013 at 18:18
// expect: exit 1
fn n<T>(): string { return type_name::<T>() }
echo type_name::<Ghost>()
